 ## Overview
-Direct Send Compositing is the simplest parallel image compositing technique for sort-last volume rendering, where each processor sends its partial image pixel data directly to the specific processor responsible for compositing that image partition [S3]. It avoids exchanging full-size images by dividing the final image into screen-space tiles, with each processor associated with compositing one tile [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Binary Swap](6889-sort-last-parallel-rendering.md), which divides communication over log2(N) rounds with paired processors swapping tiles, Direct Send performs a single stage of all-to-all communication, making it easier to implement but prone to link contention [S1, S3].
+Direct Send Compositing is the simplest parallel image compositing technique for sort-last volume rendering, where each processor sends its partial image pixel data directly to the specific processor responsible for compositing that image partition [S3]. It avoids exchanging full-size images by dividing the final image into screen-space tiles, with each processor associated with compositing one tile [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Binary Swap](6889-sort-last-parallel-rendering.md), which divides communication over $\log_2(N)$ rounds with paired processors swapping tiles, Direct Send performs a single stage of all-to-all communication, making it easier to implement but prone to link contention [S1, S3].
 
